# Docs-Coverage Playbook

Structure: **Census axes × two passes (breadth mechanical + depth judgment).** Everything
countable is *generated* (the matrix, never hand-edited); everything requiring judgment
(is this gap real, is this module actually explaining anything) is an *audit finding* with
the standard lifecycle in `00-inventory.md`.

Rule to state verbatim: **what can be counted is generated; what must be judged is a
finding.**

This is the **recurring audit** half of documentation coverage. The **authoring** half —
writing or sweeping a module — is `aidex-reference`, which runs the same census as step 1
of its workflow. Same script, two entry points: an author runs it to avoid missing things
*while writing*; this playbook runs it to find what was missed *after*.

---

## When to run

- **After a feature push** — surfaces likely outpaced their documentation.
- **After a reorganization** of `.context/references/` — the axis that used to resolve may
  have stopped seeing part of the tree.
- **When something was found by luck** — a module nobody knew was undocumented surfaced
  because an unrelated file was being deleted. That is the signal to ask *which axis would
  have found this*, and to add it.
- **Before onboarding someone** onto an area — a gap costs them a day.
- **First-time adoption** — establish a baseline and learn the true adoption rate of
  `covers:`.

---

## Preparation

**1. The profile must exist.** `.context/references/00-profile.md` declares the census axes
and their enumeration commands. If it is missing, this audit cannot run — create it via
`/aidex-reference profile`, which seeds it from the shared template.

**2. Stack-profile check.** If the project's stack does not match the template's embedded
example commands, **run an `aidex-research` spike FIRST** — "how is this stack laid out:
routes, entry points, background work, public surface" — landing notes in
`.context/research/`. Then customize the profile. **Do not guess enumeration commands for
an unfamiliar stack:** a wrong command reports full coverage of an empty set, which is the
check-that-cannot-fail failure this whole methodology exists to name.

**3. Confirm the axes with the human.** Which axes a project needs is a judgment about the
project's shape, not a fact derivable from its files. This is the only hand-maintained
input; everything downstream derives from it.

| Archetype | Axes that usually apply |
|---|---|
| Full-stack web app | routes · modules · scheduled jobs |
| Backend / API service | endpoints · modules · jobs · events consumed |
| CLI tool / toolkit | commands · sub-actions · hooks |
| Library / SDK | public exports (no visual pass at all) |
| Content / static site | pages · collections |

---

## Check matrix — two distinct passes per axis

Record the two passes **separately**. Breadth is mechanical; depth is judgment.

### Breadth (mechanical)

```bash
~/.claude/skills/aidex-reference/scripts/docs-census.sh --advisory \
  --write .context/audits/docs-coverage/<run>/coverage-matrix.md
```

**Exit 3 means the profile is not approved on this machine** — the axis commands are shell
strings and are refused until read and approved with `--trust`. That is not an error in the
audit; it is the trust gate. Inspect with `--dry-run`, and treat an axis command you did not
expect as a **P0 finding on the repo**, not a step to click through.

Three failure classes, all reported, all candidate findings:

| Class | Meaning | Usual severity |
|---|---|---|
| **gap** | item exists in code, no document declares it | P1 (critical area) / P2 |
| **phantom** | a document declares an item no census returns | P2 — the doc describes something gone |
| **contested** | two documents declare the same item | P2 — it *will* drift |

**A `BROKEN` axis is a P0 finding on the audit itself**, not a skipped row. It means the
enumeration command returns nothing, and every "100% covered" number in that run is
meaningless. Fix the command and re-run before recording anything else.

**If adoption is zero** — no module declares `covers:` — the census reports every item as a
gap and says so explicitly. Record **one** adoption finding, not N coverage findings.
Backfilling `covers:` by grepping which document mentions which module is banned: it
reintroduces the mention-vs-describe ambiguity the field exists to remove and launders a
guess into front-matter. Ownership is declared **on sweep**, per module, as each is touched.

The matrix is a generated artifact carrying a `GENERATED` header. Never hand-edit it; if a
number looks wrong, fix the profile's command or the script and re-run.

### Depth (judgment)

Breadth proves a document *claims* an item. It does not prove the document says anything
useful about it — the documentation equivalent of a test that navigates and asserts
nothing. Sample the modules covering critical areas and read them against this checklist:

- [ ] Every claim is anchored to a **symbol**, not a bare line number
- [ ] `## Verification` carries **command + real output + date** — no `- [ ]` boxes, and no
      check that passes regardless of the answer
- [ ] Verification covers **every layer the module describes** (a backend-only block on a
      full-stack module certifies half the system while reading as complete)
- [ ] States are accounted for, not just the happy path — *disabled is not absent, and
      hidden is not disabled*
- [ ] Nothing present-tense rests on a plan, an audit, or a changelog entry
- [ ] Counts and enumerations that a command returns in seconds are **not** restated in prose
- [ ] Capabilities trace to an entry point (**Rule 3′**) — otherwise it is dead code
      documented as a feature, or an entry point nobody found

Anything that fails depth is a `gap`, not coverage.

**The strongest depth instrument is not this checklist.** Spawn
`aidex-reference/agents/reference-refuter.md` against the sampled modules: it attacks claims
rather than reviewing them, with the burden of proof inverted. Use the checklist when you
need a cheap pass over many modules, the refuter when a module matters.

---

## Recording findings

- **ID convention:** `DOC-<axis>-<n>` (e.g. `DOC-routes-3`).
- **Types:** `gap` (undocumented or shallow), `bug` (a document states something false),
  `risk` (contested ownership, or an axis that may stop seeing part of the tree).
- **Severity guidance:**
  - **P0** — a `BROKEN` axis, or a document making a **false** present-tense claim about a
    money, auth or data-integrity path
  - **P1** — a critical surface or subsystem with no owning document
  - **P2** — secondary gap, phantom, or contested ownership
  - **P3** — a documented item whose depth is thin but not misleading

Escalate per the standard lifecycle (`/aidex-audit escalate <id>` → backlog, or a
remediation plan). One canonical row per finding in `00-inventory.md`.

**Record defensible gaps as waived findings, not as prose.** A module with no owning
document whose surface is genuinely explained inside another mechanism's document is a
legitimate outcome — but it is a *decision*, so it belongs in the inventory with its reason,
where the next run will not re-raise it as new.

---

## Output artifacts

- `.context/references/00-profile.md` — the keystone, **maintained** by hand (assisted on
  first run).
- `coverage-matrix.md` — **generated**, never hand-edited (carries the `GENERATED` header).
- Findings — rows in the rolling `00-inventory.md`; this run's `findings.md` is a filtered
  view.

Rule again, verbatim: **what can be counted is generated; what must be judged is a
finding.**

---

## Tips

The war stories this playbook is made of:

- **Census both axes; the blind spot of each is what the other counts.** A route census
  closed a feature topic and was structurally blind to a six-endpoint pipeline stage that
  has no route. A module census found it — and on its own first run found a second module
  described nowhere.
- **The gap was found by luck. The census is what you build so next time it is not luck.**
- **"Mentioned" is not "described", and grep cannot tell them apart.** One stage was
  mentioned in four files and described in none. This is the entire reason ownership is
  declared rather than inferred.
- **Read the lines, never the count alone.** An anchor-counting `grep -c` kept exiting 0
  while a folder reorg made it stop seeing 159 of the anchors it existed to count. If you
  move files, re-run the check and read its output, not its exit code.
- **A count that rises is a prompt to look, not a verdict.**
